  "verbalization": "Levomilnacipran, a serotonin-norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor (SNRI), is implicated in the treatment of major depressive disorder (MDD) through its effects on neurotransmitter regulation. The visible evidence suggests that Levomilnacipran may be repurposed for MDD, as it is indicated for anxiety disorders, which are often comorbid with MDD. Both Desvenlafaxine and Vortioxetine, which are also SNRIs, are indicated for MDD, and their association with Levomilnacipran implies a shared mechanism of action involving serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake inhibition. The presence of Nefazodone, another SNRI, further supports this link, as it is also indicated for MDD. The integrated evidence suggests that Levomilnacipran may exert its therapeutic effects through the modulation of neurotransmitter systems, potentially contributing to the alleviation of depressive symptoms. The strong scores across the three paths indicate a consistent and plausible mechanistic connection, though the exact extent of its efficacy in treating MDD remains to be fully elucidated.",
